# Frontend environment.
#
# The frontend reads exactly four variables, all listed below. (NODE_ENV is a
# fourth input, but the runtime sets it — never set it here.) Everything else it
# needs arrives
# at runtime from the backend over HTTP — which is the point: the two are
# separate components with separate configuration, and neither reads the
# other's secrets.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret. NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are compiled into the
# browser bundle and are readable by anyone using the site. Never put an API
# key, a database URL or a service-role token in here. Backend configuration
# lives in backend/.env.example.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development. Production values are set on the
# container, not from a file.
#
# These are checked once when the server starts (src/instrumentation.ts). In
# production a missing or malformed required value exits the process with the
# reason on stderr, so the container never becomes healthy and the deploy rolls
# back. Outside production the same problems are printed as warnings and the
# server continues. Nothing is checked during `next build`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the backend answers, for calls made on the SERVER side (route
# handlers, server components). Browser-side calls do not use this: they use
# the relative path /api, which the CDN routes to the backend on the same
# origin. See docs/delivery-plan/v2/architecture.md, "Why one origin".
#
# Local docker-compose:  http://backend:3001
# Local, running bare:   http://localhost:3001
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# The application's own public origin, used to build absolute URLs in page
# metadata and link previews. No trailing slash.
#
# Local:       http://localhost:3000
# Production:  https://legalworkflows.co.uk
#
# The bare domain, not a subdomain: the frontend and backend share one origin
# (the browser calls /api, which the CDN routes to the backend), so this is the
# origin for both. See docs/delivery-plan/v2/architecture.md, "Why one origin".
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the Corresponding Source of THIS build can be obtained — the offer
# AGPL-3.0 section 13 requires of a service used over a network, shown on
# /legal. The release pipeline sets it at build time to the public mirror at
# the deployed commit (https://github.com/<owner>/<mirror>/tree/<sha>). Leave
# unset locally: the page then links to the upstream repository instead.
#NEXT_PUBLIC_SOURCE_URL=

# Shows the "contribute this workflow" flow in the UI. Leave unset or "false"
# unless the catalogue this publishes to is one you own — see tickets 2015 and
# 2016. Publishing a client's workflow to somebody else's repository is not a
# mistake worth risking on a default.
#NEXT_PUBLIC_WORKFLOW_CONTRIBUTIONS_ENABLED=false
